\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Per-band survey characterisation of the 443 searched windows. ``Sys.'' counts unique stellar systems searched in the band, so a system observed in two bands is counted in both rows (81 systems, 88 stars in total); ``EBs'' the contributing execution blocks, attributed by the provenance of the de-duplicated windows; $\Delta \nu _{\rm  ch}$ the native channel width in MHz (median, range in brackets); ``Union'' the unique sky frequency searched in the band after merging overlapping windows; EIRP$_{5\sigma }$ the median nominal threshold across the band's windows; $\dot  {\nu }_{\rm  ceil}$ the range of drift-rate ceilings in Hz\,s$^{-1}$ per GHz of carrier frequency. The ``All'' row is computed over the whole sample, not summed down the columns: EBs and systems recur across bands and the frequency unions are disjoint by construction.}}{25}{table.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:perband}{{12}{25}{Per-band survey characterisation of the 443 searched windows. ``Sys.'' counts unique stellar systems searched in the band, so a system observed in two bands is counted in both rows (81 systems, 88 stars in total); ``EBs'' the contributing execution blocks, attributed by the provenance of the de-duplicated windows; $\Delta \nu _{\rm ch}$ the native channel width in MHz (median, range in brackets); ``Union'' the unique sky frequency searched in the band after merging overlapping windows; EIRP$_{5\sigma }$ the median nominal threshold across the band's windows; $\dot {\nu }_{\rm ceil}$ the range of drift-rate ceilings in Hz\,s$^{-1}$ per GHz of carrier frequency. The ``All'' row is computed over the whole sample, not summed down the columns: EBs and systems recur across bands and the frequency unions are disjoint by construction}{table.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The drift-rate ceiling as a physical selection function. Maximum star--planet relative acceleration, edge-on and maximised over orbital phase, as the carrier drift rate it induces (Hz\,s$^{-1}$ per GHz of carrier frequency; equivalently $\dot  \nu /\nu =\dot  v/c$), against orbital period, for host-star masses 0.09--1.8\,$M_{\odot }$ (lines) and the known planets of the planet-hosting targets (points). The shaded region lies above the drift-search ceiling, outside the searched grid: $a<0.041$\,au ($P<3.0$\,d) for a 1\,$M_{\odot }$ host. Of 41{} known planets only TRAPPIST-1\,b reaches it (\S  \ref  {sec:discussion}).}}{25}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{8}{25}{The drift-rate ceiling as a physical selection function. Maximum star--planet relative acceleration, edge-on and maximised over orbital phase, as the carrier drift rate it induces (Hz\,s$^{-1}$ per GHz of carrier frequency; equivalently $\dot \nu /\nu =\dot v/c$), against orbital period, for host-star masses 0.09--1.8\,$M_{\odot }$ (lines) and the known planets of the planet-hosting targets (points). The shaded region lies above the drift-search ceiling, outside the searched grid: $a<0.041$\,au ($P<3.0$\,d) for a 1\,$M_{\odot }$ host. Of \NExoPlanets {} known planets only TRAPPIST-1\,b reaches it (\S \ref {sec:discussion})}{figure.8}{}}
